<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lambousa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lambousa is an archaeological site on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> near Karavas (Alsancak), on the site of ancient Lapethos. The town flourished in the Roman and early Byzantine periods and was abandoned after Arab raids in the 7th century. It is famous for the Lambousa treasures, hoards of Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> found in the early 20th century, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Plates, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lambousa.jpg" alt="Lambousa" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: muffinn · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lapta_general_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
